\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} (the default) to wait.
}

}
\description{
Align protein sequences pairwise with mzLib's global affine-gap aligner, and map every residue of each sequence to its counterpart in the other, or to none.

Answers \strong{where is residue 120 of this protein in its isoform, its ortholog, or last year's
entry?} A position means something only in the sequence it was counted in, and counting the
same number along the other sequence is wrong after the first insertion or deletion. This aligns
each pair with mzLib's \code{PairwiseAligner.Align} (mzLib #1428) and maps every residue to its
counterpart in the other sequence, or to \code{NA} when it has none.
}
\details{
It is a \strong{global} alignment with affine gaps (Gotoh's form of Needleman-Wunsch), scored with
\strong{BLOSUM62}. All the pairs are aligned in one bridge call, in order, so a few thousand isoform
pairs cost one process start.

\strong{Every option you leave out is mzLib's to set.} mzLibR sends nothing for it, and the result
says what the aligner used (\code{gap_open}, \code{gap_extend}, \code{end_gaps}, \code{max_cells}). mzLib's defaults
in the pinned build are gap open 11, gap extend 1, free end gaps and a 200,000,000-cell limit:
BLASTP's gap costs for BLOSUM62, and EMBOSS needle's free end gaps.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_alignments}. \code{aligner_id} is mzLib's name for the method and every parameter,
  e.g. \code{"global-affine;BLOSUM62;open=11;extend=1;end-gaps=free"}: store it with any position you
  map, so the mapping says how it was made. \code{matrix} is always \code{"BLOSUM62"}. \code{gap_open} and
  \code{gap_extend} are the gap costs used, in BLOSUM62's half-bits; \code{end_gaps} the end-gap rule used;
  \code{max_cells} the table limit used, in cells: yours, or mzLib's default when you gave none.
  \code{record_count} counts the pairs aligned. \code{caveats} says what the score and the mapping do and do
  not mean.

  \code{records} is a data.frame with one row per pair, in input order: \code{pair_index} (\strong{1-based in R},
  so it indexes \code{pairs}); \code{source_length} and \code{target_length} in residues; \code{score}, the BLOSUM62
  score in half-bits (see the traps); \code{identical} and \code{substituted}, the paired residues that are
  the same letter and that differ, in residues; \code{source_residues_facing_gap} and
  \code{target_residues_facing_gap}, the residues with no counterpart, in residues; and
  \code{aligned_source} and \code{aligned_target}, each sequence with \code{-} wherever it faces a residue of the
  other across a gap, the same length, to print one above the other.

  \code{residue_map} is a data.frame with one row per residue of each sequence, pair by pair, each
  pair's source residues first and then its target residues: \code{pair_index} (1-based); \code{sequence},
  \code{"source"} or \code{"target"}; \code{position}, 1-based in that sequence, as in mzLib and UniProt;
  \code{residue}, as given; \code{mapped_position}, the 1-based position of its counterpart in the other
  sequence, **\code{NA} when it faces a gap, never the nearest residue**; and \code{mapped_residue}, the
  counterpart's letter (\code{NA} facing a gap), a different letter from \code{residue} on a substitution.
  It is \code{NULL} with \code{residue_map = FALSE}.

}
\section{Reading one position}{


\code{\link{proteins_map_source_to_target}} and \code{\link{proteins_map_target_to_source}} look positions up for one
pair; \code{\link{proteins_alignments}} splits a result into one \code{mzlibr_alignment} per pair for them. They
read the residue map mzLib's \code{TryMapSourceToTarget} and \code{TryMapTargetToSource} filled, and compute
nothing.

}
\section{The traps}{


**\code{score} is not an identity, a bit score or an E-value.** It is a raw sum of BLOSUM62 scores in
half-bits, less the gaps, and it grows with length. Compare scores only between alignments with
the same \code{aligner_id}. mzLibR computes no percent identity, because there are several
definitions: divide \code{identical} by the length you mean.

\strong{It is a global alignment.} Every residue of both sequences is placed, paired or facing a gap.
With free end gaps a short sequence is placed on the best-scoring stretch of a long one, even
when it has no real match there.

\strong{One optimal alignment, chosen the same way every time.} Where two alignments score the same,
mzLib's traceback picks one, always the same one. DYNC1I2 isoform 2B is a real case: canonical V76
and V82 are both V, either could pair with the isoform's V76, and mzLib pairs V82.

\strong{Always check the residue at a mapped position} before carrying a modification across: a
phosphosite on S is not a site on A. \code{mapped_residue} sits beside \code{residue} for that.

}
