\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{pairs}}{string[]; required; range \code{>= 1 pair; each line exactly one tab}. Read from stdin (not an option): one pair per line, the source sequence, a tab, the target sequence, as one-letter residue codes. Each sequence reaches mzLib exactly as given, case and all. Either sequence may be empty, but not both, because a blank line is skipped. One row per line, in input order, duplicates included. Sequences always travel on stdin, never argv: two titin isoforms are about 70,000 characters, past argv's 32 KB ceiling on Windows.}
\item{\code{gap_open} (wire \code{--gap-open})}{int; in \strong{half-bits (BLOSUM62 score units)}; default \code{11}; range \code{>= 0 (mzLib refuses a negative value)}. The cost of opening a gap, charged once per gap. A gap of length k costs gap-open plus k times gap-extend, BLAST's convention. Absent: nothing is sent and mzLib's PairwiseAligner default applies, which is 11 at d10017ce (BLASTP's default with BLOSUM62).}
\item{\code{gap_extend} (wire \code{--gap-extend})}{int; in \strong{half-bits (BLOSUM62 score units)}; default \code{1}; range \code{>= 1 (mzLib refuses 0 or less)}. The cost of each residue in a gap, the first included. Absent: mzLib's default, 1 at d10017ce.}
\item{\code{end_gaps} (wire \code{--end-gaps})}{string; default \code{free}; range \code{free | charged (case-insensitive)}. free: a gap at either end of either sequence costs nothing, so one sequence may overhang the other (a signal peptide, a longer C-terminus) without penalty, as EMBOSS needle scores end gaps by default. charged: every gap costs. mzLib's PairwiseAligner.FreeEndGaps, true or false. Absent: mzLib's default, free (true) at d10017ce. The spelling is mzLib's own, from its aligner id.}
\item{\code{max_cells} (wire \code{--max-cells})}{int; in \strong{cells}; default \code{2e+08}; range \code{1 to 2147483591 (Array.MaxLength; mzLib refuses anything outside)}. The largest dynamic-programming table mzLib will build, (source length + 1) x (target length + 1) cells of one byte each. A larger pair is refused rather than allowed to exhaust memory. Absent: mzLib's default, 200,000,000 cells (about 200 MB) at d10017ce.}
\item{\code{residue_map} (wire \code{--no-residue-map})}{flag; default \code{FALSE}. Leave out the residue map (residue_map is then null). The pairs table is unchanged. For a batch where only the scores and counts are wanted: the map has one row per residue of both sequences.}
}
