\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item score is a raw substitution-matrix score in BLOSUM62's half-bit units, summed over the alignment: it grows with length and is not a bit score, an E-value or a percent identity. Compare scores only between alignments with the same aligner_id.
\item Global alignment: every residue of both sequences is in the alignment, paired or facing a gap. With end gaps free (mzLib's default) an overhang at either end costs nothing, so a short sequence is placed on the best-scoring stretch of a long one even when it has no real match there.
\item A residue facing a gap has no counterpart: its mapped_position is null, never the nearest residue.
\item One optimal alignment is returned. Where several score the same, mzLib's traceback prefers a residue pair, then a gap in the target, then a gap in the source, so the same input always gives the same alignment; a residue beside an indel may sit on the other side of the gap in another aligner's equally good answer. DYNC1I2 isoform 2B lacks six residues of the canonical sequence: V76 and V82 of the canonical are both V, either could pair with the isoform's V76, and mzLib pairs V82.
\item mzLib refuses only the gap characters '-' and '.'. Any other character BLOSUM62 does not name (selenocysteine U, pyrrolysine O, J, a digit, a space, a bracket) is scored as X, so pass bare one-letter sequences. Letters are compared case-insensitively; aligned_source, aligned_target and the residue columns keep the case given.
\item Time and memory grow with the product of the two lengths: mzLib builds a (source length + 1) x (target length + 1) table of one byte per cell and refuses a pair larger than max_cells. Two identical sequences free of X and * are aligned residue for residue without the table, so max_cells does not limit them.
\item Each stdin line is one pair, aligned in input order; blank lines are skipped. mzLib's aligner takes no thread count, so pairs are aligned one after another.
}
}
\section{Performance}{
Every call starts one bridge process, which costs a .NET start-up before any work. 
