\section{Not yet verified}{
The spec records these as open. They are listed rather than hidden:

\itemize{
\item A substitution matrix other than BLOSUM62 is not offered. mzLib can read one (SubstitutionMatrix.Parse(name, TextReader), NCBI's text format), but it needs a name for the aligner id, which the bridge would have to invent, and the units of every score would then be that matrix's. Whether to add \code{--matrix} FILE, and where its name comes from, is open.
\item mzLib scores any character BLOSUM62 does not name as X, digits and spaces included, so a sequence with a stray modification bracket or a number aligns without complaint. Whether PairwiseAligner.Align should refuse non-letters, as it refuses '-' and '.', is an mzLib question, not raised upstream yet.
\item Pairs are aligned one at a time: PairwiseAligner takes no degree of parallelism, so the wire has no \code{--threads} (PARALLELISM.md). A batch aligner with one (for proteome-wide ortholog or isoform maps) would be an mzLib addition.
\item residue_map is not validated by tools/check_verbs.py, which checks only 'columns' and 'bulk'. The bridge test ADeletion_LeavesItsResiduesUnmappedAndMapsAcrossTheGap pins its column order.
\item Not yet ported to mzLibRust or mzLibR; the binding names above are proposals.
}
}
